'''transfusion''' (trȧns-fū′zho̤n), ''n.'' [< F. ''transfusion'' = Sp. ''transfusion'' = Pg. ''transfusão'' = It. ''transfusione'', < L. ''transfusio(n-)'', a pouring from one vessel into another, < ''transfundere'', pp. ''transfusus'', pour from one vessel into another: see ''transfuse''.] 1. The act of transfusing, or of pouring, as a liquid, out of one vessel into another; hence, in general, transmission; transference.

{{smaller block|Poesy is of so subtile a spirit that in the pouring out of one language into another it will all evaporate; and if a new spirit be not added in the ''transfusion'', there will remain nothing but a "caput mortuum."
{{right|''Sir J. Denham.''}}}}

{{smaller block|Their wild, imaginative poetry, scarcely capable of ''transfusion'' into a foreign tongue.
{{right|''Prescott'', Ferd. and Isa., i. 8.}}}}

2. In ''med.'', the transmission of blood from one living animal to another, or from a human being or one of the lower animals into a human being, with the view of restoring the vigor of exhausted subjects or of replacing the blood lost by hemorrhage; also, the intravenous injection of other liquids, such as milk or saline solutions, in order to restore the circulating fluid to its normal volume, as after severe hemorrhage. {{smaller|This operation is of old date, but seems to have ended generally in failure until about 1824, the chief cause of failure probably being the want of due precautions to exclude the air during the process.}}

{{smaller block|Mem. that at the Epiphanie, 1649, when I was at his house, he then told me his notion of cureing diseases, &c., by ''transfusion'' of bloud out of one man into another, and that the hint came into his head reflecting on Ovid's story}}

readings.c1s3:
{{smaller block|of Medea and Jason. {{right|''Aubrey'', Lives (Francis Potter).}}}}

{{smaller block|'''Direct''' or '''immediate transfusion,''' the transmission of blood directly from the veins of the donor into those of the recipient.—'''Indirect''' or '''mediate transfusion,''' the injection into the veins of the recipient of blood which has been first allowed to flow into a bowl or other vessel and there defibrinated.—'''Peritoneal transfusion,''' the injection of defibrinated blood into the peritoneal cavity, with a view to its absorption into the system.}}

'''transfusionist''' (trȧns-fū′zhọn-ist), ''n.'' [< ''transfusion'' + ''-ist''.] One who is skilled in the surgical process of transfusion; one who advocates that process.

{{smaller block|The early ''transfusionists'' reasoned, in the style of the Christian Scientists, that the blood is the life.
{{right|''Pop. Sci. Mo.'', XXXIV. 808.}}}}

'''transfusive''' (trȧns-fū′siv), ''a.'' [< L. ''transfusus'', pp. of ''transfundere'', transfuse, + ''-ive''.] Tending or having power to transfuse.

'''transfusively''' (trȧns-fū′siv-li), ''adv.'' So as to transfuse; in a transfusive manner. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|The Sunne . . . his beames ''transfusively'' shall run
Through Mars his Sphere, or loves benigner Star.
{{right|''Heywood'', Hierarchy of Angels, p. 278.}}}}

readings.c2s1:
{{smaller block|'Tis time my hard-mouth'd coursers to control,
Apt to run riot, and ''transgress'' the goal.
{{right|''Dryden'', tr. of Ovid's Metamorph., xv. 669.}}}}

{{smaller block|The Furies, they said, are attendants on justice, and if the sun in heaven should ''transgress'' his path they would punish him.
{{right|''Emerson'', Compensation.}}}}

Hence—2. To overpass, as some law or rule prescribed; break or violate; infringe.

{{smaller block|It is evident that Aristotle ''transgressed'' the rule of his own ethics.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Religio Medici, i. 55.}}}}

{{smaller block|Whilst men continue social units, they cannot ''transgress'' the life principle of society without disastrous consequences.
{{right|''H. Spencer'', Social Statics, p. 488.}}}}

3†. To offend against (a person); disobey; thwart; cross; vex.

{{smaller block|I never
Blasphem'd 'em, uncle, nor ''transgress'd'' my parents.
{{right|''Fletcher'', Bonduca, iv. 2.}}}}

{{smaller|='''Syn.''' 2. ''Infringe upon, Encroach upon'', etc. (see ''trespass'', ''v. i.''), pass, transcend, overstep, contravene.}}

'''II.''' ''intrans.'' To offend by violating a law; sin.

{{smaller block|The troubler of Israel, who ''transgressed'' in the thing accursed.
{{right|1 Chron. ii. 7.}}}}

{{smaller block|I would not marry her, though she were endowed with all Adam had left him before he ''transgressed''.
{{right|''Shak.'', Much Ado, ii. 1. 260.}}}}

'''transgressible''' (trȧns-gres′i-bl), ''a.'' [< ''transgress'' + ''-ible''.] Liable to transgression, or capable of being transgressed. ''Imp. Dict.''

'''transgression''' (trȧns-gresh′o̤n), ''n.'' [< F. ''transgression'' = Pr. ''transgressio'' = Sp. ''transgresion'', ''tragresion'' = Pg. ''transgressão'' = It. ''transgressione'', ''trasgressione'', < L. ''transgressio(n-)'', a passing over, transposition, also a transgression of the law, < ''transgredi'', pp. ''transgressus'', pass over: see ''transgress''.] The act of transgress-

readings.c2s2:
ing; the violation of any law; disobedience; infringement; trespass; offense.

{{smaller block|Whosoever committeth sin trangresseth also the law: for sin is the ''transgression'' of the law. {{right|1 John iii. 4.}}}}

{{smaller block|They that are in the flesh . . . live in sin, committing many actual ''transgressions''.
{{right|''Book of Common Prayer'', Baptism of those of Riper Years.}}}}

=<b>Syn.</b> ''Sin'', ''Trespass'', etc. (see ''crime''), infraction, breach.

'''transgressional''' (trȧns-gresh′ọn-a̤l), ''a.'' [< ''transgression'' + ''-al.''] Pertaining to or involving transgression. [Rare.]

{{smaller block|Forgive this ''transgressional'' rapture; receive my thanks for your kind letter. {{right|''Bp. Burnet'', Life, I. p. xlix.}}}}

'''transgressive''' (trȧns-gres′iv), ''a.'' [< LL. ''transgressivus'', that goes or passes over, < L. ''transgredi'', pass over: see ''transgress.''] Inclined or apt to transgress; faulty; sinful; culpable.

{{smaller block|Permitted unto his proper principles, Adam perhaps would have sinned without the suggestion of Satan, and from the ''transgressive'' infirmities of himself might have erred alone, as well as the angels before him.
{{right|''Sir T. Browne'', Vulg. Err., i. 10.}}}}

'''transgressively''' (trȧns-gres′iv-li), ''adv.'' 1. In a transgressive manner; by transgressing.—2. In ''geol.'', unconformably.

{{smaller block|Let us suppose, for example, that a mountain range consists of upraised Lower Silurian rocks, upon the upturned and denuded edges of which the Carboniferous Limestone lies ''transgressively''. ''A. Geikie'', Encyc. Brit., X. 371.}}

'''transgressor''' (trȧns-gres′ọr), ''n.'' [< ME. ''transgressour'', < OF. ''transgresseur'' = Pr. ''transgressor'' = Sp. ''transgresor'', ''trasgresor'' = Pg. ''transgressor'' = It. ''trasgressore'', < L. ''transgressor'', an infringer,

